            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Trust, reliance, and confident assurance in God's character, promises, and the redemptive work of Jesus Christ, encompassing both intellectual belief and relational devotion.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Hebrews 11:1\",\"Ephesians 2:8-9\",\"Romans 4:3-5\",\"John 3:16\",\"James 2:14-26\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Faithfulness, allegiance, or loyal obedience, emphasizing the Greek term pistis as fidelity or loyalty to Christ as Lord.\",\"basis\":[\"Romans 1:5\",\"Galatians 5:22\",\"Revelation 2:10\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Intellectual assent or cognitive agreement to specific theological propositions and historical facts.\",\"basis\":[\"James 2:19\",\"Hebrews 11:6\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A spiritual force, tangible substance, or mechanism used to speak things into existence or manifest divine promises (often found in Word of Faith theology).\",\"basis\":[\"Hebrews 11:1\",\"Mark 11:22-24\",\"Romans 10:8\"],\"prominence\":\"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While the broad consensus defines faith as relational trust and reliance on God, theological nuances exist. Protestant traditions emphasize faith as the sole instrument of justification, while Catholic traditions emphasize faith formed by love. Recent academic discussions, particularly regarding Pauline theology, heavily debate the translation of pistis Christou as either faith in Christ or the faithfulness of Christ.\"}",
